      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I’ve had the same issues with ipv6.\n\nI ended up disabling my ipv6 connection entirely on my computers network adapters (Ethernet & Wi-Fi) to get it to work.\n\nI just uploaded a new avatar last night and there were multiple versions in the package. One with a huge download and another with a small download. The lighter one downloaded just fine with ipv6 enabled, but the larger one wouldn’t download at all.\n\nLiterally turned off ipv6 and wouldn’t you know it, the full one would download.\n\nI also had issues from a previous session where no prints would load at all when ipv6 was enabled.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2024-02-29T17:25:15.935Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "1. Open VRChat\n\n2. Either Load into your home world (Image doesnt load for the Home World) or load into an Error World, seemingly does this when it feels like it and/or after a cache cleanse for a second session.\n\n3. Read output log to see \"Unable to secure SSL Connection\" in attempt to load Avatar and/or world.\n\n4. Attempt to fix Wifi Issue, doesnt seem to work, even if allowing ports and checking everything.\n\n5. Try to fix priority settings in Killer Intelligence Center (I.e change VRChat priority to 1, aka highest priority) \n\n6. Seems to work until next session, where it breaks again. Computer restart doesnt seem to work to fix this, neither does a clean reinstall.\n\nVRChat Support tends to be confused and say its a Wifi Issue when it comes to the SSL connection, but really its been found that either VRChat itself or EAC hates ipv6. I think due to the rarity of this bug (I have found others with the same problem) it is never really looked into, and I believe it should. It seems to also be kind of spiratic, and almost unpredictable if and when it happens. I really want to play this game again properly and want to see this get patched for everyone's sake.\n\nMAJOR EDIT: This is actually caused by ipv6 ever since the most recent few updates! I believe this should be something to focus on fixing!",
